let __v_191 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lamb chops\",\"lamb chop\",\"lamb cutlets\",\"lamb cutlet\"]},\"Lamb chops are a hot-pan job, 3 to 4 minutes a side for pink in the middle, or 5 if you want them through, then 3 minutes resting: a chop is thin, so the pan has to be properly hot before they go in and the fat edge wants standing on to render. In the oven they take 12 to 15 minutes at 200C (180C fan). Salt them just before they go in, not before.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lamb shank\",\"lamb shanks\"]},\"Braise lamb shanks: brown them in a heavy pan, then cook covered at 160C (140C fan) for 2 to 2.5 hours with stock, onion, carrot and a spoon of tomato, until the meat gives way to a fork. They are the cut a slow cooker suits, on low for 6 to 8 hours. Cook them the day before if you can, because the sauce sets and the fat comes off in one piece.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"gammon steak\",\"gammon steaks\",\"gammon\"]},\"Gammon steak is cooked through, not pink: 4 to 5 minutes a side in a hot pan, or 15 to 20 minutes under a grill, turning once. If it is thick or you want it milder, simmer it 15 minutes in water first, then dry it and fry it. It needs no salt - it is already cured - and a spoon of honey or marmalade in the pan at the end glazes it.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"beef brisket\",\"brisket\",\"brisket joint\",\"rolled brisket\"]},\"Brisket is a long cook, not a quick one: in the oven at 150C (130C fan), covered, for 3 to 4 hours, or about 1 hour per 500g, with a splash of stock in the tin, until a fork slides in with no pull. Season it heavily the night before if you can. It slices better after 20 minutes resting, and it is the one cut that is better the next day.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lamb kofta\",\"kofta\",\"koftas\",\"lamb kofte\",\"kofte\"]},\"Kofta is minced lamb shaped by hand: mix 500g of mince with a grated onion squeezed dry, a handful of parsley, cumin, cinnamon and salt, then shape fingers around a skewer and grill them 10 to 12 minutes, turning, on a barbecue or under the grill, or bake them on a tray in the oven at 200C (180C fan) for 15 to 18 minutes, or fry them 3 to 4 minutes a side. The onion is what keeps them soft; squeeze it or they fall apart.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"duck legs\",\"duck leg\",\"duck thighs\",\"duck thigh\"]},\"Roast duck legs at 160C (140C fan) for 1.5 to 2 hours, skin side up on a rack over a tin, until the skin is crisp and the meat pulls off the bone: the long slow heat is what renders the fat under the skin, and a hot oven just burns it. Prick the skin, salt it well, and do not cover them.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"liver\",\"lambs liver\",\"chicken liver\",\"chicken livers\",\"calves liver\",\"pig liver\"]},\"Liver is a hot, fast job: pat it dry, flour it lightly if you like, and fry it 2 to 3 minutes a side in a hot pan with butter, no more, until it is brown outside and still pink in the middle. Past that it goes grainy and bitter. Soaking it 30 minutes in milk takes the edge off a strong one, and it wants onions cooked slowly first, not alongside.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"kidneys\",\"kidney\",\"lamb kidneys\",\"pig kidneys\",\"calves kidneys\"]},\"Kidneys are a hot, fast job like liver: halve them, cut out the white core, and fry them 2 minutes a side in a very hot pan with butter, or 3 to 4 minutes in total if they are sliced. Past that they go rubbery. In a stew they are the opposite - an hour and a half, low - which is why the same meat has two timings.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"whole duck\",\"a whole duck\",\"roast duck\",\"roasting duck\"]},\"Roast a whole duck at 200C (180C fan) for 20 minutes, then at 160C (140C fan) for 20 minutes per 500g, pricking the skin all over and salting it first: the hot start crisps the skin and the lower heat renders the fat under it. Rest it 15 minutes. If you have time, pour a kettle of boiling water over it and let it dry before it goes in - that is what makes the skin crackle.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"whole fish\",\"a whole fish\",\"whole salmon\",\"whole trout\",\"whole sea bass\",\"whole mackerel\"]},\"A whole fish is scored on both sides and roasted at 200C (180C fan) for 20 to 25 minutes for a 1kg fish, or about 10 minutes for every 2.5cm through the thickest part, with lemon and herbs in the belly and a little oil rubbed into the skin. It is done when the eye has gone white and the fin pulls out easily. Sit it on sliced onion or fennel so it does not stick.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"scallops\",\"scallop\",\"king scallops\"]},\"Scallops take 1.5 to 2 minutes a side in a very hot pan and no more: pat them dry, salt them, and put them in when the oil just starts to smoke, then leave them alone until the edge is golden brown. Wet scallops steam instead of browning, and a crowded pan does the same. The orange coral is good to eat and wants half the time.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"monkfish\"]},\"Monkfish takes 12 to 15 minutes at 200C (180C fan), or 3 to 4 minutes a side in a hot pan: it is dense and holds together, so a rasher of bacon or a slice of prosciutto wrapped round it and a squeeze of lemon at the end is all it needs. It is done when it turns opaque white right through, and unlike cod it will not fall apart if you give it another minute.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"squid\",\"squid rings\",\"calamari\"]},\"Squid is either 2 minutes or 45: rings, or tubes scored in a diamond pattern, into a very hot pan for 1 to 2 minutes until they curl and turn opaque, or into a stew or a slow braise for 45 minutes to an hour - anything in between is rubber. Pat them dry first, or they steam instead of browning.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"crab\",\"dressed crab\",\"brown crab\"]},\"A cooked crab is picked, not cooked again: twist off the claws, crack them with the back of a knife, and pull the white meat out of the shell, keeping the brown meat from the body separate because it is the stronger tasting of the two. A live crab goes into boiling salted water for about 15 minutes and cools in the water.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"rabbit\",\"rabbit stew\",\"wild rabbit\"]},\"Rabbit wants either a long braise or a fast roast: jointed and browned, then covered with stock and cooked at 150C (130C fan) for 1.5 to 2 hours, it comes away from the bone; the saddle roasted at 200C for 20 to 25 minutes stays moist. It is very lean, so it needs bacon, wine or a lid to keep it from drying out.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"lamb neck\",\"neck of lamb\",\"lamb neck fillet\",\"neck fillet\"]},\"A lamb neck is a braise: brown it, then cook it covered at 150C (130C fan) with stock and onions for 2 to 2.5 hours until the meat comes off the bone, or 8 hours on low in a slow cooker. Neck fillet is the quick cut of the same name - 4 to 5 minutes a side in a hot pan, rested for 5.\"]},{\"$tuple\":[{\"$tuple\":[\"oxtail\",\"oxtail stew\",\"ox tail\"]},\"Oxtail needs about 3 hours: brown the pieces, then cook them covered at 150C (130C fan) with stock, onion, carrot and a spoon of tomato until the meat comes away from the bone - the bone and the gelatine in it are what give the sauce its body. Skim the fat off the top before serving, and it is better made the day before.\"]}]}")?;
frame.store("_EXTRA_BASICS", __v_191.clone())?;
