let __v_484 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"chicken\",{\"$tuple\":[\"Chicken or turkey stock simmers 1.5 to 2 hours at the barest bubble, and 3 if you want it stronger. Skim the grey foam off in the first twenty minutes and keep the lid off so it never boils hard, then strain it through a sieve. Cool it fast in a sink of cold water, which takes about an hour, and it keeps 4 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\",\"Cover the picked carcass with cold water, add an onion, a carrot, a bay leaf and a few peppercorns, bring it to the barest simmer and skim the grey foam off in the first twenty minutes; 2 hours is plenty, 3 if you want it stronger. Strain it, cool it in a sink of cold water for about an hour, and it keeps 4 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\"]}],[\"brown\",{\"$tuple\":[\"Beef, lamb or pork stock wants 3 to 4 hours at the barest simmer, after half an hour of browning the bones in a hot oven. It should set like jelly when it is cold, which is what tells you the bones were worth the time.\",\"Brown the bones with the onion and carrot halved at 220C (200C fan) for 30 minutes first, because the colour and most of the flavour come from that browning. Then cover them with cold water, add a bay leaf and a few peppercorns and simmer at the barest bubble for 3 to 4 hours, skimming the foam in the first twenty minutes. Strain it, and it keeps 4 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\"]}],[\"fish\",{\"$tuple\":[\"Fish stock takes 20 to 30 minutes at a bare simmer and no longer - after that the bones turn it bitter and gluey. Skim the foam, don't stir it, and strain it through kitchen paper.\",\"Fish stock is the quick one: fish bones and trimmings with an onion, a carrot, a bay leaf and a few peppercorns, covered with cold water and simmered 20 to 30 minutes and no longer. Strain it through a sieve lined with kitchen paper, and it keeps 2 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\"]}],[\"veg\",{\"$tuple\":[\"Vegetable stock simmers 30 to 40 minutes, and longer than that the sweetness goes muddy. Leave out the strong greens - cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower, beetroot - and season it at the end.\",\"Vegetable stock needs no bones at all: an onion, a carrot, a stick of celery, a bay leaf and a few peppercorns in cold water, simmered 30 to 40 minutes. Leave out cabbage, broccoli, cauliflower and beetroot, which turn it bitter and sour, put soft herbs in at the end, and it keeps 4 days in the fridge or 3 months frozen.\"]}]]}")?;
frame.store("STOCK_METHODS", __v_484.clone())?;
let __v_485 = Value::from("Homemade stock keeps 4 days in the fridge and 3 months in the freezer. Cool it fast, get it into the fridge within 2 hours, freeze it in bags laid flat or in an ice cube tray for spoonfuls, and bring it back to a rolling boil before you eat it. A cube or a carton follows the date on the pack.");
frame.store("STOCK_KEEP", __v_485.clone())?;
let __v_486 = Value::from("A stock cube makes about 450ml of stock, one cube to a pint of water, and they are salty, so taste before you add any salt. Crumble it into hot water rather than dropping it in whole, and use half for a small pan of rice or gravy. Fresh stock in a tub beats a cube whenever the stock is the point of the dish.");
frame.store("STOCK_CUBES", __v_486.clone())?;
let __v_487 = Value::from("A stock cube keeps its flavour for about a year in a dry cupboard, and the date on the box is the honest guide. Once the foil is open, wrap the rest or keep it in a sealed tub, because a cube that takes in damp goes hard and clumps; a soft one is still fine to use.");
frame.store("STOCK_CUBE_KEEP", __v_487.clone())?;
let __v_488 = Value::from("Skim the grey foam off in the first twenty minutes with a spoon or a ladle, and keep the pot at the barest simmer with the lid off. The foam is blood and protein: it tastes flat and it is what clouds a stock, and after twenty minutes there is nothing left to skim. Don't stir the pot, or the fat mixes back in.");
frame.store("STOCK_SKIM", __v_488.clone())?;
let __v_489 = Value::from("In a slow cooker, cover the bones and vegetables with cold water and cook on low for 6 to 8 hours, or high for 3 to 4, then strain it. Brown the bones in the oven first or the flavour is thin, and keep the liquid an inch below the lid. A slow cooker stock is weaker than a simmered one, so reduce it by a third before you freeze it.");
frame.store("STOCK_SLOW", __v_489.clone())?;
let __v_490 = Value::from("Flavour comes from browning: roast the bones and the vegetables at 220C (200C fan) for 30 minutes before they go in the pot, and add a spoon of tomato puree or a few dried mushrooms. Don't let it boil, which drives the aroma off, and salt it only at the end. Reduce the strained stock by half and you have a stronger one for the freezer.");
frame.store("STOCK_FLAVOUR", __v_490.clone())?;
let __v_491 = Value::from("Stock is flavour, not medicine: it has some minerals and collagen from the bones, but the evidence for the joint and skin claims is thin, and a stock cube is mostly salt. Homemade with little or no salt is the useful version of it, and it is the cheapest way to make a soup or a stew taste of something.");
frame.store("STOCK_HEALTH", __v_491.clone())?;
let __v_492 = Value::from("A litre of stock to 300g of risotto rice, added a hot ladle at a time; about 500ml for a soup for two; and enough to cover the bones in the pot by an inch. Whatever is left freezes in a bag, so make more than you need.");
frame.store("STOCK_AMOUNT", __v_492.clone())?;
